ITT definition: \cf2 the average difference in outcomes between users assigned to treatment and users assigned to control, regardless of whether the treatment was actually delivered or engaged with.\cf0 \
\
\pard\tx560\tx1120\tx1680\tx2240\tx2800\tx3360\tx3920\tx4480\tx5040\tx5600\tx6160\tx6720\sl324\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf2 ITT is the default because assignment is randomized and fully under platform control. It captures the real-world, operational impact of launching the feature, for example, delivery failures, non-compliance, and logging noise.\
\
If researchers are asking the causal effect of the actual usage/treatment, ITT is not the ideal option. In this case researchers may tend to analyze LATE with IV. Before analyzing LATE, researchers have to examine if assignment affects receipt (relevance), assignment affects outcomes only through receipt (exclusion), assignment is randomized (independence), and no defiers. \cf0 \
